bulletproofs — provenance note
===============================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `frost`/
`voprf`/`adaptor`/`bn254` modules — self-contained crypto modules not
folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

bulletproofs — the protocol design follows Bünz, Bootle, Boneh, Poelstra,
Wuille, Maxwell, "Bulletproofs: Short Proofs for Confidential
Transactions and More", IEEE S&P 2018 (eprint.iacr.org/2017/1066), §3
(the Inner-Product Argument, `ipa.zig`) and §4.1/§4.2 (the single-value
range proof, `rangeproof.zig`). This is a public academic paper; the
paper's TEXT (the protocol description/equations being followed) is not
itself a copyrightable implementation, and no source code accompanying
the paper was consulted or ported — see CONVENTIONS.md §5's merger-
doctrine note (the same posture this repo's other spec-derived modules,
e.g. `frost`/`voprf`/`threshold_ecdsa`, take for their own IETF/academic
sources).

dalek-cryptography/bulletproofs (Rust, MIT, crates.io `bulletproofs`
4.0.0) and its transcript crate `merlin` 3.0.0 (MIT) are the wire
reference since 2026-09-30. Their source was read for the facts a
compatible implementation has to reproduce: Merlin's STROBE-128 subset and
message framing (`transcript.zig`), dalek's transcript labels and their
order, the identity checks in its verifier, the generator derivation
(`GeneratorsChain` over SHAKE256, `B_blinding` from SHA3-512;
`generators.zig`) and the proof byte layout (`rangeproof.zig`, `ipa.zig`).
These are interface facts, re-expressed in Zig over `std`; no Rust code was
translated line for line, and the prover keeps its own structure (OS
entropy for the blinding, no transcript RNG, constant-time ladders). Before
that date the module used a self-made SHA-512 transcript and SHA-512
generators and was not wire-compatible with anything; the change is
recorded in CHANGELOG.md.

The dalek and merlin crates are also run, as black boxes, by
`tools/dalek/` to produce the vectors in `src/interop_vectors.zig`.

`std.crypto.ecc.Ristretto255` (group operations, `fromUniform`'s
Elligator2-based one-way map per RFC 9496 §4.3.4) and
`std.crypto.ecc.Ristretto255.scalar`/`.scalar.Scalar` (the shared
Edwards25519 scalar field: `add`/`sub`/`mul`/`mulAdd`/`neg`/`reduce64`/
`rejectNonCanonical`, and `Scalar.fromBytes(..).invert().toBytes()` for
constant-time inversion), `std.crypto.core.keccak`, `std.crypto.hash.sha3`
(SHA3-512, SHAKE256) (MIT, part of Zig itself) are used via their public APIs; no new curve, field, or hash
primitive is implemented here. The sibling `frost`/`voprf` modules'
`root.zig` doc comments were consulted for this repo's existing
Ristretto255-usage conventions (scalars-as-raw-`[32]u8`, `meta` tag
shape) — no code from those modules was copied (this module has no
sibling-module dependency, `meta.deps = .{}`). ⚠ That last parenthesis is
stale as of the ct25519 constant-time work: `meta.deps` is now
`.{"ct25519"}` — the constant-time secret-scalar ladder behind
`scalarvec.mulCt`. Still no code copied from a sibling.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: complete.** Generators (`generators.zig`), the Fiat-Shamir
transcript (`transcript.zig`), scalar/vector + multi-scalar-mult helpers
(`scalarvec.zig`), and both proof structs' byte codecs
(`ipa.InnerProductProof`, `rangeproof.RangeProof`) are implemented and
tested for real, and so are the two zero-knowledge cores —
`ipa.proveIpa`/`.verifyIpa` and `rangeproof.prove`/`.verify`. They are no
longer `@panic("TODO(fable/core): ...")` stubs; the crypto pass landed and
`gate.core_implemented` is `true`.

⚠ This paragraph said "scaffold" until 2026-09-09, long after that was
true. It is recorded rather than silently rewritten because a status line
that under-reports is the same defect as one that over-reports: this one
told a reader the module was unusable, while the neighbouring "Caveats"
bullet told them the prover leaked timing it no longer leaks. Both were
found by the same audit.
